set data = data || '{"relevantReading":[{"label":"Opioids and renal function","source":"The Journal of Pain","url":"https://www.jpain.org/article/S1526-5900(03)00872-1/fulltext"},{"label":"Opioid Metabolism and Effects of Cytochrome P450","source":"Pain Medicine","url":"https://academic.oup.com/painmedicine/article-abstract/10/suppl_1/S20/1914905"}]}'::jsonb
where id = 'q_sba_opioid_renal_copd_fentanyl_001';

-- ===== Retroactive knowledge box + relevant reading: q_statistics_001 =====
update questions
set data = data || '{"explanation":"<p>Statistical literacy is directly examined in the FFPMRCA. Key concepts include measures of central tendency and spread, statistical test selection for parametric versus non-parametric data, and clinical epidemiology metrics such as NNT and NNH.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>A. TRUE</strong> — NNT = 1 / ARR (absolute risk reduction). If a treatment reduces the risk of an event from 40% to 30%, the ARR is 10% and the NNT is 10. Lower NNT values indicate more effective treatment. This formula is fundamental and frequently tested.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>B. TRUE</strong> — In a perfect normal (Gaussian) distribution, the mean, median, and mode are all identical, coinciding at the centre of the symmetrical bell curve. This is a defining characteristic of the normal distribution.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>C. FALSE</strong> — In a positively skewed (right-skewed) distribution, there is a long tail to the right caused by high-value outliers. These outliers pull the mean upward relative to the median, so mean > median in positive skew. A helpful mnemonic: the mean is dragged in the direction of the skew.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>D. TRUE</strong> — The Mann-Whitney U test is used to compare two independent groups when data are non-parametric. It is the non-parametric equivalent of the independent samples t-test. For paired non-parametric data, the Wilcoxon signed-rank test is used instead.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>E. TRUE</strong> — A type II error is failing to detect a true effect (false negative). The power of a study (1 – β) increases with sample size. A larger sample provides greater ability to detect a true difference, which is why underpowered studies fail to reach statistical significance even when a real effect exists.</p><div style=\"margin-top:18px\"><div style=\"border:1px solid var(--border-strong);border-radius:14px;overflow:hidden\">\n  <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:14px;padding:12px 18px;background:var(--coral)\">\n    <span style=\"font-weight:700;color:#fff;font-size:14.5px\">NNT, Hypothesis Testing and Statistical Power</span>\n    <div style=\"display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:7px;flex-shrink:0\">\n      <svg width=\"14\" height=\"14\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"10\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"12\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"8\" x2=\"12.01\" y2=\"8\"/></svg>\n      <span style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#fff;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.55);border-radius:999px;padding:3px 9px\">Knowledge</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background:var(--surface-warm)\"><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;\"><div style=\"max-width:480px;margin:12px auto 0\"><div style=\"position:relative;padding-bottom:56.25%;height:0;overflow:hidden;border-radius:10px;border:1px solid #EDE3D7\"><iframe src=\"https://www.youtube.com/embed/_Pyi12dn4Kw\" title=\"Video: statistics for medical students\" style=\"position:absolute;top:0;left:0;width:100%;height:100%;border:0\" allow=\"accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture; web-share\" allowfullscreen loading=\"lazy\"></iframe></div></div></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Absolute Risk Reduction, NNT and NNH</div><p style=\"margin:0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\"><strong>Absolute risk reduction (ARR)</strong> is the difference in event rates between a control and treatment group. The <strong>number needed to treat (NNT)</strong> is the number of patients who must receive a treatment for one additional patient to benefit, and is the inverse of the ARR. The <strong>number needed to harm (NNH)</strong> is the equivalent calculation for an adverse outcome.</p><table style=\"margin:12px 0 0\"><tr><th>Metric</th><th>Formula</th><th>Worked example</th></tr><tr><td><strong>ARR</strong></td><td>Control event rate &minus; treatment event rate</td><td>40% &minus; 30% = 10%</td></tr><tr><td><strong>NNT</strong></td><td>1 &divide; ARR</td><td>1 &divide; 0.10 = 10</td></tr><tr><td><strong>NNH</strong></td><td>1 &divide; absolute risk increase (ARI)</td><td>If ARI = 5%, NNH = 1 &divide; 0.05 = 20</td></tr></table><p style=\"margin:10px 0 0;font-size:12.5px;font-style:italic;color:var(--stone)\">A lower NNT indicates a more effective treatment; a higher NNH indicates a safer one.</p></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Null Hypothesis and Alternative Hypothesis</div><p style=\"margin:0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\">The <strong>null hypothesis</strong> states that there is no real difference or effect &mdash; any difference observed is assumed to be due to chance alone. The <strong>alternative hypothesis</strong> states that a real difference or effect does exist. A hypothesis test is designed to determine whether there is enough evidence to reject the null hypothesis in favour of the alternative.</p><p style=\"margin:10px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\"><strong>Example:</strong> when testing whether a new analgesic reduces pain scores compared with placebo &mdash; the null hypothesis is that there is no difference in pain scores between the analgesic and placebo groups; the alternative hypothesis is that the analgesic produces a real reduction in pain scores compared with placebo. If the study&rsquo;s p-value falls below the pre-set significance threshold (&alpha;, conventionally 0.05), the null hypothesis is rejected in favour of the alternative.</p></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Type I and Type II Errors</div><p style=\"margin:0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\">A <strong>Type I error (&alpha;)</strong> is rejecting a true null hypothesis &mdash; a false positive, concluding there is an effect when there is not. A <strong>Type II error (&beta;)</strong> is failing to reject a false null hypothesis &mdash; a false negative, concluding there is no effect when one truly exists. &alpha; is conventionally set at 0.05 (a 5% accepted risk of a false positive) before a study begins.</p><table style=\"margin:12px 0 0\"><tr><th></th><th>Null hypothesis actually true</th><th>Null hypothesis actually false</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Test rejects the null hypothesis</strong></td><td>Type I error (&alpha;) &mdash; false positive</td><td>Correct decision &mdash; power (1&minus;&beta;)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Test fails to reject the null hypothesis</strong></td><td>Correct decision (1&minus;&alpha;)</td><td>Type II error (&beta;) &mdash; false negative</td></tr></table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Statistical Power and What Reduces It</div><p style=\"margin:0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\"><strong>Power (1&minus;&beta;)</strong> is the probability that a study correctly detects a true effect when one exists &mdash; i.e. correctly rejects a false null hypothesis. An underpowered study risks a Type II error even when a real difference is present.</p><table style=\"margin:12px 0 0\"><tr><th>Factor</th><th>Effect on power</th></tr><tr><td>Small effect size (e.g. testing whether 5mg is more effective than 2.5mg of a drug)</td><td>Reduces power &mdash; small true differences are harder to detect than large ones (e.g. testing 100mg vs 5mg)</td></tr><tr><td>High standard deviation / data variability</td><td>Reduces power &mdash; more &ldquo;noise&rdquo; obscures a true signal</td></tr><tr><td>Low alpha (a stricter significance threshold)</td><td>Reduces power &mdash; a smaller accepted false-positive rate makes it harder to reach significance</td></tr><tr><td>Non-parametric statistical tests</td><td>Generally less powerful than their parametric equivalents when data are normally distributed</td></tr><tr><td>Small sample size</td><td>Reduces power (conversely, increasing sample size increases power)</td></tr></table></div></div>\n</div></div>","relevantReading":[{"label":"Statistics for medical students","source":"Geeky Medics","url":"https://geekymedics.com/statistics-for-medical-students/"}]}'::jsonb
where id = 'q_statistics_001';
